 ],
 "literal": "Digging a well in saline soil daily, / the pure white cloth washed by the washerwoman, / sitting in the street of pollen and manure dust to absorb dirt, / to the noble lord with a flower garland who fulfilled the needs of many, / there is indeed no one, alas! In the battle, / while his excellent red eyes burn with rage, / he who has the nobility to shield with his shield.",
 "translation": "Like a pure white cloth, washed by a washerwoman who digs a well in saline soil every day, which now lies in the street of pollen and dry manure, absorbing dirt—so lies the noble lord of the flower garland, who once fulfilled the needs of many. Alas, there is no one now in the battle to protect him, he who possessed the nobility to shield others with his shield when his excellent red eyes burned with rage.",
 "modern": "உப்பு நிலத்தில் நாள்தோறும் கிணறு தோண்டி, வண்ணாத்தி துவைத்த தூய்மையான வெண்ணிற ஆடை, மகரந்தமும் எருவும் நிறைந்த தெருவில் விழுந்து அழுக்கடைவது போல, பலருடைய தேவைகளை நிறைவேற்றிய மலர்மாலை அணிந்த தலைவன் போர்க்களத்தில் தூசியில் கிடக்கிறான். போரில் அவனுடைய சிறந்த சிவந்த கண்கள் கோபத்தால் புகைய, தன் கேடயத்தைக் கொண்டு பிறரைக் காக்கும் சால்புடைய அவனுக்கு, இப்போது போர்க்களத்தில் அவனைக் காக்க ஒருவரும் இல்லையே!",
 "explanation": "This poem is a lament by the poet Avvaiyar on the battlefield, belonging to the tumpai tiṇai (warfare) and the theme of pāṇ pāṭṭu (bard's song). The poet compares the fallen hero to a pristine white cloth that was washed with great effort by a washerwoman who had to dig a fresh well in salty soil daily. Just as that painstakingly cleaned cloth now lies discarded in the dusty street absorbing dirt and manure, the noble lord who once wore fresh flower garlands and generously fulfilled everyone's needs now lies soiled on the battlefield. In the heat of battle, when his red eyes burned with rage, he had the nobility to shield others with his shield. Yet now, as he lies wounded or dead, there is no one left to shield or protect him.",
